<article class="env-remark" id="0BCT">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BCT">Remark <span data-tag="0BCT">31.17.3</span>.</a> It is not clear how to characterize the non-Noetherian local rings $(A, \mathfrak m)$ whose punctured spectrum is affine. Such a ring has a finitely generated ideal $I$ with $\mathfrak m = \sqrt{I}$. Of course if we can take $I$ generated by $1$ element, then $A$ has an affine puncture spectrum; this gives lots of non-Noetherian examples. Conversely, it follows from the argument in the proof of Lemma <a href="/tag/0BCR" data-tag="0BCR">31.17.1</a> that such a ring cannot possess a nonzerodivisor $f \in \mathfrak m$ with $H^0_ I(A/fA) = 0$ (so $A$ cannot have a regular sequence of length $2$). Moreover, the same holds for any ring $A'$ which is the target of a local homomorphism of local rings $A \to A'$ such that $\mathfrak m_{A'} = \sqrt{\mathfrak mA'}$. </p>
</article>